steps.body:
A graph is a set of nodes joined by edges. Number the nodes `0` to `n - 1` and keep, for each node, the list of its neighbors. That is an adjacency list: a `List<List<Integer>>` with one `new ArrayList<>()` per node.

```java
List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
}
adj.get(u).add(v);   // edge u to v
adj.get(v).add(u);   // undirected: add the reverse too
```

An undirected edge (a friendship, a two-way road) goes in both lists. A directed edge (a follow, a one-way street) goes only in `u`'s list. The degree of a node is how many edges touch it: `adj.get(u).size()`. In a directed graph that size is the out-degree, and the in-degree needs its own `int[]` counter.

Neighbors land in the order the edges were read. Sort each list with `Collections.sort` so traversals and printed output follow a fixed order.

Avoid `List<Integer>[] adj = new ArrayList[n]`. That array uses a raw type, so javac prints an unchecked note, and `new ArrayList<Integer>[n]` does not compile at all. A list of lists has neither problem.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int m = sc.nextInt();
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            int u = sc.nextInt();
            int v = sc.nextInt();
            adj.get(u).add(v);
            adj.get(v).add(u);
        }
        for (List<Integer> neighbors : adj) {
            Collections.sort(neighbors);
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.println(i + ": " + adj.get(i) + " degree " + adj.get(i).size());
        }
    }
}

