steps.body:
This is the grid DFS from the Depth-First Search lesson with one change: the neighbors come from the adjacency list, `adj.get(node)`, instead of four directions, so there are no bounds to check. Keep a `boolean[] visited` so each node is entered once. Without it, every undirected edge leads straight back to the node you came from, and the recursion never ends.

```java
static int dfs(int node, List<List<Integer>> adj, boolean[] visited) {
    visited[node] = true;
    int size = 1;
    for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
        if (!visited[next]) {
            size += dfs(next, adj, visited);
        }
    }
    return size;
}
```

Mark the node before you look at its neighbors. The for-each loop unboxes each `Integer` neighbor into an `int`. Returning `1` plus what each recursive call found gives the number of nodes this call reached.

In an undirected graph, a connected component is a largest group of nodes that can all reach each other. To count them, loop over every node and launch a DFS from each one that is still unvisited, just as you counted islands in the Depth-First Search lesson. Each launch finds one whole component, so the number of launches is the component count, and each return value is that component's size.

Recursion depth grows with the component, so the limit of about 2,000 frames from the Recursion lesson applies here too.

