steps.body:
BFS works on a graph just as it did on a grid: the queue hands back nodes in rings, every node 1 edge away, then 2, then 3. In an unweighted graph that order gives the fewest edges from the start to every node. The neighbors come from the adjacency list `adj`, the queue holds plain node numbers, and `dist` is a one-dimensional `int[]`, so a single `Arrays.fill` works.

```java
int[] dist = new int[n];
Arrays.fill(dist, -1);   // -1 means not visited yet
Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
dist[start] = 0;
queue.offer(start);
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int node = queue.poll();
    for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
        if (dist[next] == -1) {
            dist[next] = dist[node] + 1;
            queue.offer(next);
        }
    }
}
```

The `dist` array does two jobs. It holds the answers, and `dist[v] == -1` is the visited check, so you need no `boolean[]`. Set the start's distance before the loop, or the start looks unvisited and gets a distance of 2 later.

Set a node's distance when you enqueue it, not when you poll it, or the same node can enter the queue several times. Nodes still at `-1` at the end are unreachable, which is exactly what to print for them. DFS cannot replace BFS here: it finds *a* path, not the shortest one.

