steps.body:
With weighted edges (distances, prices) the fewest edges is not always the cheapest route. Dijkstra's algorithm finds the cheapest one when no weight is negative. Store each edge as `{neighbor, weight}` in a `List<List<int[]>>`, and keep a `PriorityQueue<int[]>` of `{node, distance}` that always hands back the closest node.

```java
PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));
int[] dist = new int[n];
Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);   // infinity
dist[start] = 0;
pq.offer(new int[]{start, 0});
```

Each pass polls `{u, d}`. If `d > dist[u]`, a cheaper route to `u` was found after this entry went in, so skip the stale entry with `continue`. Otherwise relax every edge: if `d + w < dist[v]`, store the new distance and offer `{v, dist[v]}`. Java's `PriorityQueue` cannot lower an entry's key, so offering a fresh entry and skipping stale ones is the standard approach.

Two gotchas. Compare with `Integer.compare`, never `a[1] - b[1]`, which overflows for large values. And relax only from a polled distance, which is always finite: `Integer.MAX_VALUE + w` wraps to a negative number that looks like a shortcut. If real totals can pass about 2 billion, store distances as `long`. At the end, print `-1` for any node still at `Integer.MAX_VALUE`.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int m = sc.nextInt();
        List<List<int[]>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            int u = sc.nextInt();
            int v = sc.nextInt();
            int w = sc.nextInt();
            adj.get(u).add(new int[]{v, w});
            adj.get(v).add(new int[]{u, w});
        }

        int[] dist = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));
        dist[0] = 0;
        pq.offer(new int[]{0, 0});
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int[] top = pq.poll();
            int u = top[0];
            int d = top[1];
            if (d > dist[u]) {
                continue;
            }
            for (int[] edge : adj.get(u)) {
                int v = edge[0];
                int w = edge[1];
                if (d + w < dist[v]) {
                    dist[v] = d + w;
                    pq.offer(new int[]{v, dist[v]});
                }
            }
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int shown = dist[i] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : dist[i];
            System.out.println(i + ": " + shown);
        }
    }
}

